🔥 Downstream Collectors (Summary)

Used inside groupingBy() / partitioningBy() to decide what happens inside each group.

🔥 1. Collecting (most important foundation)
🔹 Collectors.toList()

Collects elements into a List

Collectors.toList()
🔹 Collectors.toSet()

Removes duplicates automatically

Collectors.toSet()
🔹 Collectors.toCollection()

Custom collection type

Collectors.toCollection(ArrayList::new)
Collectors.toCollection(TreeSet::new)

PTR
Correct 👍 — you don’t need to write Collectors.toList() explicitly.

List<String> words = List.of("a", "a", "b", "b", "b");

groupingBy(
    s -> s.charAt(0),
    Collectors.toList()
)

a → [a, a]
b → [b, b, b]

groupingBy(
    s -> s.charAt(0),
    Collectors.toSet()
)

a → [a]
b → [b]

🔥 2. Counting / Size related
🔹 Collectors.counting()

Counts elements in each group

Collectors.counting()

👉 Result type: Long

Map<Character, Long> result =
    list.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
            s -> s.charAt(0),
            Collectors.counting()
        ));

{
  a = 2,
  b = 2
}

🔥 3. Mathematical reductions
🔹 summingInt / Long / Double
Collectors.summingInt(Employee::getSalary)

🔹 averagingInt / Long / Double
Collectors.averagingInt(Employee::getSalary)

🔹 minBy / maxBy
Collectors.maxBy(Comparator.comparing(Employee::getSalary))
Collectors.minBy(Comparator.comparing(Employee::getSalary))
(Refer Employee.java for the the maxBt demo)
👉 returns Optional<T>

🔥 4. Mapping (VERY important for groupingBy)
🔹 Collectors.mapping()

Transforms values inside a group

Collectors.mapping(
    Employee::getName,
    Collectors.toList()
)

👉 Example:
Dept → List of Employee names


List<String> list = List.of("apple", "ant", "bat", "ball");
Map<Character, List<Integer>> result =
    list.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
            s -> s.charAt(0),
            Collectors.mapping(
                String::length,
                Collectors.toList()
            )
        ));
✔ Instead of storing words → storing transformed values
{
  a = [5, 3],
  b = [3, 4]
}


🔥 5. Joining (for Strings)
🔹 Collectors.joining()
Collectors.joining()
🔹 With delimiter
Collectors.joining(", ")
🔹 With prefix/suffix
Collectors.joining(", ", "[", "]")

List<String> list = List.of("apple", "banana", "cherry");

String result = list.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.joining(", "));

combining it with groupingBy

Map<Character, String> result =
    list.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
            s -> s.charAt(0),
            Collectors.mapping(
                s -> s.toUpperCase(),
                Collectors.joining(", ")
            )
        ));


🔥 6. Reducing (custom logic)
🔹 Collectors.reducing()

Very flexible aggregation

Collectors.reducing((a, b) -> a + b)

Or with identity:

Collectors.reducing(0, x -> x, Integer::sum)


🔥 7. Group transformation helper
🔹 Collectors.collectingAndThen()

Post-process result

Collectors.collectingAndThen(
    Collectors.toList(),
    List::size
)

👉 Convert List → count, or modify final result

🔥 8. Special (partitioning support)
🔹 partitioningBy downstream options

Same collectors apply:

Collectors.partitioningBy(
    x -> x > 10,
    Collectors.counting()
)
🔥 9. Common combinations (VERY IMPORTANT)
🔹 Group + count
groupingBy(key, counting())
🔹 Group + list of names
groupingBy(
    Employee::getDept,
    mapping(Employee::getName, toList())
)
🔹 Group + max salary
groupingBy(
    Employee::getDept,
    maxBy(comparing(Employee::getSalary))
)



Collectors.averagingInt()
Collectors.summingInt()
Collectors.maxBy()
Collectors.minBy()
Collectors.mapping()
Collectors.collectingAndThen()
Collectors.counting()
